  "metaTitle": "Video on a Medical Practice Website: Captions and Schema",
  "metaDescription": "How to add video to a medical practice website with captions, a transcript and VideoObject markup so it helps patients and can appear in search.",
  "answer": "Put each video on a page patients would actually visit, add captions and a text transcript, and describe the video with VideoObject structured data so Google can understand it. Captions make it usable without sound, the transcript gives search engines text to read, and the markup is optional help, not a ranking guarantee.",
  "author": "Liam Costello",
  "publishDate": "2026-10-08T09:00:00Z",
  "dateModified": "2026-10-08",
  "readMinutes": 7,
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Why a practice would add video"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "A short video of the provider introducing themselves, a tour of the office, or a walkthrough of what a first visit looks like can calm a nervous patient better than a paragraph. Patients choosing a new doctor or therapist want a sense of who they will meet, and video answers that quickly."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Video also carries risks that plain text does not: it can be slow to load, it is unusable without captions for people who are deaf or hard of hearing or watching on a muted phone, and search engines cannot read what is spoken unless you give them text. The steps below deal with each."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Step one: decide where the video lives"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Put the video on the page that matches its topic, near the text it supports. A provider introduction belongs on that provider's page. An office tour belongs on the location page. Google's video guidance asks that a video be placed on a page where it is a main part of the content or clearly supports it, and that each video have its own crawlable page or a clearly associated page."
    },
    {
      "type": "ul",
      "items": [
        "Use a video player embedded in the page's HTML, so Google can find it.",
        "Avoid placing the only copy of important information inside the video.",
        "Keep the file or embed reasonably small, and do not autoplay with sound.",
        "Host on a platform you control or a service your team can maintain, and know who owns the account."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Step two: add captions"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Captions are the text of the spoken words, synchronized with the video. The W3C accessibility guidance on audio and video explains why they matter: people who are deaf or hard of hearing need them, and so does anyone watching in a quiet waiting room. Use a human-reviewed caption file rather than relying only on automatic captions, because medical names, drug names and local place names are often wrong in auto-captions."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Step three: publish a transcript"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "A transcript is the full text of the video on the page, including who is speaking and any important sound or on-screen text. It helps patients who prefer to read, it works for screen reader users, and it gives search engines real words to index. Put it in an expandable section below the video, or in the page body if the video is short."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Step four: add VideoObject structured data"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "VideoObject is a type of structured data that tells Google about a video on the page. Google's documentation lists the properties it needs, including a name, a description, a thumbnail URL and an upload date, and it recommends others, such as a content URL or embed URL and a duration. It is added as JSON-LD in the page, usually by your developer or website platform."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Property",
        "What to provide"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "name",
          "The video's title in plain words, such as Meet Dr. Rivera"
        ],
        [
          "description",
          "One or two sentences describing what the video shows"
        ],
        [
          "thumbnailUrl",
          "A working image URL for the video's still"
        ],
        [
          "uploadDate",
          "The date the video was published"
        ],
        [
          "contentUrl or embedUrl",
          "The video file or the player's embed address"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The markup must match what is visible on the page. Do not describe a video that is not there or add claims that are not in the video. Structured data is not a guarantee that a video will appear as a rich result. For general background, see our explanation of [schema markup for a medical practice website](/guides/schema-markup-for-a-medical-practice-website-explained)."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Step five: keep patients and privacy safe"
    },
    {
      "type": "ul",
      "items": [
        "Do not film or show patients, charts, screens or anything that could identify a patient without a proper written authorization. See our [testimonial compliance checklist](/guides/patient-testimonial-compliance-checklist).",
        "Do not make outcome promises or clinical claims in the script.",
        "Check what the video host loads on your page. Some embeds set cookies or trackers, which matters for [HIPAA-safe tracking](/guides/hipaa-safe-tracking-for-a-medical-website)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Check it before you publish"
    },
    {
      "type": "ol",
      "items": [
        "Play the video on a phone with sound off. Are the captions readable and correct?",
        "Confirm the transcript matches what is said, including names and terms.",
        "Test the page speed before and after adding the video.",
        "Run the page through the Rich Results Test to confirm the structured data parses.",
        "Watch it all the way through for anything you would not want shown publicly."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "q": "Do I need structured data for a video to appear in Google?",
      "a": "No. Google can discover a video on a page without markup, but VideoObject structured data gives it clearer information and can make a video eligible for video-related results. It is a helpful extra, and neither it nor any other step guarantees visibility."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are automatic captions good enough?",
      "a": "Usually not by themselves. Automatic captions often misspell medical terms, names and places. Review and correct them, or upload your own caption file, so patients and screen readers get an accurate version of what is said."
    },
    {
      "q": "Should I upload videos to YouTube or host them on my site?",
      "a": "Either can work. YouTube is convenient and widely supported. Whichever you choose, embed the video on a relevant page of your own site, add a transcript there, and know who owns the account. Check what trackers an embed loads."
    },
    {
      "q": "How long should a practice video be?",
      "a": "Short enough that a patient will watch it, often one to two minutes for a provider introduction or office tour. There is no required length. Put the key message early and make the transcript available for those who prefer to read."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I put patient testimonials on video?",
      "a": "Only with proper written authorization and attention to compliance rules, which vary. Patient identity and health information are sensitive, and a video makes them more visible. Review our testimonial checklist and your own compliance advisor before publishing any patient on camera."
    }
  ],
